Task: Migration 063 — extend rental_payments (Phase 1, Tier 1)

WHAT CHANGED
Added ten nullable columns to public.rental_payments: statement_path,
management_fees, letting_fees, lease_fees, sundry_fees, other_outgoings,
net_received, extracted, confidence, fees_confirmed_at. Plus a partial index
rental_payments_fees_confirmed_idx on (property_id, payment_date) WHERE
fees_confirmed_at IS NOT NULL.

Files: supabase/migrations/063_rental_payment_statements.sql (new)

THE CORE WHY
rental_payments was the only financial-evidence table with nowhere to store its
document, so rent was the one figure reaching the tax pack without evidence.
The larger defect is the agent-fee deduction: computed as
weeks * weekly_rent * management_fee_pct, it can only reproduce a recurring
percentage. On statement OWN10905 that recovers the $242 management fee and
misses $1,210 letting fee, $33 lease fee and $8.80 bank charges — $1,251.80 of
genuine deductions — while carrying no estimate disclosure.

Extended rather than replaced with a rental_statements table: rows already
exist in production and one statement maps to one payment row in practice.
All columns nullable, so no data migration.

Two invariants are documented in the migration header because they are not
recoverable from any single file:
  - `amount` is GROSS rent, the assessable figure. net_received is where the
    disbursed figure belongs.
  - other_outgoings is NEVER a deduction. Third-party costs the agent paid
    deduct via their own invoice, the only path that classifies them correctly
    (blinds are Division 40, not an immediate deduction). A deduction path from
    this column would double-count.

DEVIATION FROM SPEC
Spec FR2 said the same migration would redefine the storage-object functions.
Split into 063 (columns) and 064 (functions) instead: 063 is applied before
task 2 begins, and editing an applied migration is forbidden by the project
conventions. Also matches the existing 055/058 precedent. Spec and plan
amended.

